artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a stablecoin system whose value is intended to remain pegged to some target unit of account, and whose backing is provided by a collateral asset under one of the following two regimes:

1. Proxy collateralization: the stablecoin is backed by a separate, on-chain crypto-asset whose market value is exogenous to the stablecoin system.
2. Self collateralization: the stablecoin is backed by an internally issued secondary token of the same system, whose value depends endogenously on the stablecoin's stability and on user expectations.

In both regimes, solvency is enforced by a margin-call mechanism: when the value of posted collateral falls below a contractually specified threshold relative to the outstanding stablecoin liabilities, collateral is liquidated (or additional collateral is required to be posted) so as to restore the required collateralization ratio.

Standard analyses of margin calls assume that the market for the collateral asset is sufficiently deep and liquid that the required quantity of collateral can be sold or acquired in a timely manner without significantly affecting its price. Consider instead settings in which one or more of the following hold:

(a) the collateral market has small total capitalization;
(b) the collateral market is young, with limited trading history and thin order books;
(c) the collateral market is illiquid in the sense that liquidating a non-trivial fraction of outstanding collateral within the time window allowed by the margin-call protocol causes substantial adverse price impact;
(d) market expectations about the future value of the collateral asset deteriorate, so that prospective buyers withdraw and selling pressure increases.

Formulate a mathematical model of the stablecoin, its collateral, the margin-call protocol (including triggering thresholds, liquidation auctions or forced sales, and any feedback between liquidations and collateral price), and the trading environment, that captures conditions (a)–(d). Within such a model, determine whether there exist margin-call mechanisms (parameter choices, auction designs, liquidation schedules, or alternative triggers) such that the stablecoin remains solvent and maintains its peg with high probability under stress, or alternatively prove impossibility results identifying regimes of market size, liquidity, and expectation dynamics in which no margin-call mechanism can guarantee robustness.

